So every single one of these videos that you're seeing is made in four simple steps.

01:43.060 --> 01:45.440
So the first one is that you give Claude the ingredients.

01:45.780 --> 01:48.140
It then gives you a storyboard that you can approve,

01:48.140 --> 01:53.060
and Claude goes away and builds that physical storyboard into a sequence for you.

01:53.320 --> 01:56.960
Then it adds the sound design, the music, and the voiceover.

01:57.240 --> 02:02.100
Now we're going to show you a few different examples that get more and more advanced as we go.

02:02.300 --> 02:07.160
So this Spotify example is the easiest level of using Claude to create motion graphics.

02:07.460 --> 02:09.100
And just look how clean this is.

02:09.100 --> 02:30.640
The logo builds itself.

02:30.780 --> 02:32.080
It turns into a music player.

02:32.220 --> 02:33.580
The player turns into a playlist.

02:33.920 --> 02:35.220
And we land on the phone.

02:35.220 --> 02:40.840
So the first thing we need to do to create something of this quality is you need to make a folder.

02:41.160 --> 02:43.120
So this is going to be our Spotify folder.

02:43.520 --> 02:45.040
So in this folder goes the skill.

02:45.200 --> 02:48.440
And I'll leave a link in the description to that skill so you can go and download it.

02:48.600 --> 02:50.280
And you're just going to want to upload it in here.

02:50.520 --> 02:55.980
Now this skill has all of the knowledge that we have built up over the last two weeks of testing this

02:55.980 --> 02:57.860
to help us get to the best results.

02:58.180 --> 03:02.300
The next thing is a high resolution image of your logo and any other assets.

03:02.300 --> 03:07.300
And then you can also upload any of the screenshots of maybe inside the app and the user interface.

03:07.540 --> 03:11.020
Tip number one is use real high quality asset references.

03:11.340 --> 03:17.000
Reason being is quality that you input into the model equals quality of the output that you're going to get.

03:17.160 --> 03:22.660
If you don't give quality references to Claude, Claude will then go and create a fake version

03:22.660 --> 03:24.580
that just looks like a fake app.

03:24.660 --> 03:26.820
And we obviously don't want that look for your brand.

03:27.020 --> 03:28.780
And if you don't upload a high quality logo,

03:28.780 --> 03:32.740
it will try to create that logo from scratch by creating shape layers.

03:32.960 --> 03:36.920
And a lot of the time it can look pretty close, but it's never actually the exact logo.

03:37.100 --> 03:42.580
And it's important that you have a proper high resolution image of the logo so it can custom animate it.

03:42.820 --> 03:45.600
So now we're going to give Claude access to this file.

03:45.840 --> 03:47.340
And you can do that by just clicking here.

03:47.440 --> 03:48.900
And then you can select the file.

03:49.260 --> 03:52.100
And now Claude is talking to that file.

03:52.240 --> 03:54.220
It understands what is inside it.

03:54.340 --> 03:57.560
Now this is a critical part of your motion graphics with Claude.

03:57.560 --> 04:02.720
Before you go and animate anything, you need to ask Claude to create a numbered storyboard

04:02.720 --> 04:08.020
that basically breaks out the sequence of all of the things that are going to happen in the video.

04:08.340 --> 04:12.440
Then you can go in and approve that before anything happens.

04:12.760 --> 04:17.440
Otherwise, you're just going to waste your credits going through and having Claude build animations for you

04:17.440 --> 04:19.380
when you're not approving the process.

04:19.600 --> 04:22.680
So you can see here Claude has now built this storyboard for us.

04:22.820 --> 04:27.040
Now we can go back and forth with Claude and ask for specific tweaks and changes

04:27.040 --> 04:29.660
so that we can get the exact kind of storyboard that we want.

04:29.820 --> 04:33.280
And this is where you start directing it before Claude starts to do any animations.

04:33.640 --> 04:34.860
This is my third tip for you.

04:34.900 --> 04:36.100
And this is also a critical one.

04:36.280 --> 04:38.520
So once you're happy with your storyboard and you've got that locked in,

04:38.760 --> 04:41.300
before you click enter and let Claude go away and do the animation,

04:41.760 --> 04:47.300
you want to instruct Claude that every shot should transform into the next one.

04:47.300 --> 04:52.640
So each of the storyboard frames should seamlessly transition into the next frame.

04:52.780 --> 04:57.300
That way you get one seamless motion where it's all tied together very creatively.

04:57.740 --> 05:02.520
And it doesn't feel like you then just got like individual shots stacked on top of each other.

05:02.640 --> 05:07.420
If you don't direct this, Claude will basically create those individual shots.

05:07.540 --> 05:11.400
And then to blend them together, it will do something called a cross dissolve.

05:11.400 --> 05:16.280
And I'll show on screen right here what a cross dissolve actually looks like.

05:16.340 --> 05:17.560
And you can see it looks cheap.

05:17.660 --> 05:18.560
It doesn't look impressive.

05:18.840 --> 05:24.600
But then if you actually instruct it to not use cross dissolves and have it seamlessly transition,

05:24.940 --> 05:27.360
you get way more impressive results like this over here.

05:27.440 --> 05:30.980
So we want to specifically say like there should be no cross dissolves in here.

05:31.280 --> 05:33.320
There should always be some sort of motion,

05:33.520 --> 05:36.620
whether that is an object or a shape transforming into one another,

05:36.620 --> 05:41.440
because then it keeps the viewer's eye looking at something on the screen.

05:41.620 --> 05:45.380
And as a byproduct, keeps your viewer engaged, which then makes higher quality videos.

05:45.560 --> 05:48.300
So when you're looking at your storyboard, you want to ask yourself the question,

05:48.440 --> 05:50.620
how can I turn this shape into this shape?

05:50.740 --> 05:53.020
And then describe how you want that to happen.

05:53.120 --> 05:55.540
And maybe describe how you want the transitions to work.

05:55.860 --> 05:58.320
And Claude can then direct off of your feedback.

05:58.380 --> 06:01.300
Once you're happy with it and approved, you can click enter.

06:01.560 --> 06:04.320
And then Claude will go and start making that animation for you.

06:04.320 --> 06:06.680
And this one took about eight to ten minutes to come through.

06:07.020 --> 06:09.640
Just look how like these three lines join up into a circle.

06:09.780 --> 06:13.520
The circle then becomes a square and the square opens up into the music player.

06:13.740 --> 06:19.240
Now, pro tip number four, we want to tell Claude to keep whatever is happening in the center of the frame.

06:19.440 --> 06:21.660
And when I say center, I mean the dead center.

06:22.020 --> 06:24.540
So your eyes, when you're watching the motion graphic,

06:24.820 --> 06:29.300
never has to like look and bounce around the screen to understand what's going on.

06:29.300 --> 06:35.620
It allows you to keep your eyes locked in in a certain position and all the effects happen around it.

06:35.700 --> 06:37.800
This is going to improve the quality of your animations a lot.

06:37.960 --> 06:43.540
Now, tip number five, with motion graphics, there should always be some sort of motion happening that you can see here, for example.

06:43.840 --> 06:50.780
It doesn't need to be crazy or over the top action, but there should at least be some camera motion or something moving in the frame.

06:50.920 --> 06:52.580
Otherwise, it can feel a bit stale and slow.

06:52.840 --> 06:53.700
Now, give this a watch.

06:53.700 --> 06:58.680
One of these versions has no sound on the left, and now we have sound on the right.

